DATA = [{'filename': 'como-aprovechar-la-luz-natural-en-casa.html', 'title': 'How to Make Better Use of Natural Light at Home', 'desc': 'Learn how to make better use of natural light at home through window access, curtains, furniture placement, reflective surfaces, wall colors, and thoughtful room planning.', 'intro': 'Natural light can transform how a home feels, make rooms appear more open, and reduce the need for artificial lighting during part of the day. Simple changes around windows and furniture can make a noticeable difference.', 'category': 'HOME', 'body': '<h2>In This Article</h2>\n<p>Natural light can change how a home feels, making rooms appear more open and reducing the need for artificial lighting during part of the day. Improving it often requires only better window access, suitable curtains, thoughtful furniture placement, and surfaces that help distribute brightness.</p>\n<h2>Introduction</h2>\n<p>Natural light can change how a home feels, making rooms appear more open and reducing the need for artificial lighting during part of the day. Improving it often requires only better window access, suitable curtains, thoughtful furniture placement, and surfaces that help distribute brightness. The most effective improvements usually come from observing the room at different times of day, removing unnecessary obstacles, and making small changes that support daily use without creating new clutter.</p>\n<h2>Keep Windows Clear</h2>\n<p>One of the simplest ways to improve natural light is to avoid placing tall or bulky furniture directly in front of windows. A sofa back, shelving unit, large plant, or decorative screen can interrupt daylight before it reaches the rest of the room. Rearranging a few pieces may have more impact than buying new lighting.</p>\n<p>Check the window from several angles. Sometimes an object blocks only part of the glass but still creates a darker zone because it sits in the path of the strongest daylight. Low furniture and visually lighter pieces can often preserve function without reducing access to light.</p>\n<p>Keep the area around the window easy to clean. Dust, condensation, and outdoor residue can gradually reduce clarity, especially on glass that is difficult to reach. A simple cleaning routine helps the window perform as intended.</p>\n<h2>Choose Curtains and Blinds Carefully</h2>\n<p>Window coverings need to balance light, privacy, temperature, and comfort. Heavy curtains can be useful in some rooms but may block a large portion of daylight when gathered at the sides. Consider whether a lighter fabric, wider curtain rod, or different opening method would expose more glass during the day.</p>\n<p>Layered solutions can provide flexibility. A light privacy layer combined with a heavier curtain may allow more daylight when privacy is still needed. The right option depends on the room, orientation, climate, and what can be seen from outside.</p>\n<p>Blinds should also be adjusted rather than simply left in one fixed position. Tilting slats or raising them partially can direct light deeper into the room while reducing glare in areas where screens or work surfaces are used.</p>\n<h2>Arrange Furniture Strategically</h2>\n<p>Furniture placement affects how daylight travels. Large pieces positioned close to windows can create shadows, while open layouts allow light to reach more surfaces. Before moving everything, identify the darkest areas and the objects that may be contributing to them.</p>\n<p>Place activities that benefit from daylight near brighter zones when practical. A reading chair, desk, or dining table may work better closer to a window, provided glare, heat, and privacy remain comfortable.</p>\n<p>Do not force a layout simply to maximize brightness. Circulation, storage, acoustics, and daily habits also matter. The best arrangement improves light without making the room inconvenient.</p>\n<h2>Use Reflective Surfaces With Restraint</h2>\n<p>Light-colored walls and certain smooth surfaces can help distribute daylight, but the goal is not to turn every room into a reflective box. Choose finishes that suit the style of the home and consider how they behave under both daylight and artificial lighting.</p>\n<p>Mirrors can redirect views and brightness when placed thoughtfully. Position them where they reflect useful light or an attractive part of the room rather than clutter, a television screen, or an area you would prefer not to emphasize.</p>\n<p>Glossy furniture and metallic accessories can add small highlights, but too many reflective materials may create glare. Use them selectively and evaluate the result at different times of day.</p>\n<h2>Choose Wall Colors Intentionally</h2>\n<p>Lighter colors generally reflect more light, but small rooms do not have to be white. Soft neutrals, pale warm colors, or light muted tones can create brightness while maintaining personality. The undertone of a color may also change noticeably depending on window orientation.</p>\n<p>Test paint samples on more than one wall and observe them in morning, afternoon, and evening light. A color that looks bright in the store may appear very different in a north-facing or shaded room.</p>\n<p>Darker colors can still work as accents or in rooms where a more intimate atmosphere is desired. Natural-light planning should support the purpose of the room, not impose one design style.</p>\n<h2>Pay Attention to Window Orientation</h2>\n<p>The direction a window faces influences the amount, color, and timing of daylight. East-facing rooms often receive stronger morning light, while west-facing rooms may become brighter and warmer later in the day. South- and north-facing conditions vary by hemisphere and local context, so observe the actual room rather than relying only on general rules.</p>\n<p>Use this information when assigning functions. A room with gentle morning light may suit one activity, while a room that receives intense afternoon sun may need better glare or heat control.</p>\n<p>Seasonal changes matter too. Sun angles shift during the year, so a room that feels bright in one season may behave differently in another.</p>\n<h2>Reduce Visual Barriers</h2>\n<p>Large partitions, dense shelving, and tall decorative pieces can interrupt sight lines and daylight in open spaces. Where privacy is not essential, lower furniture or more open designs may allow light to pass more freely.</p>\n<p>This does not mean every divider should be removed. Storage and zoning are still important. The goal is to decide which barriers are necessary and which are simply making the room darker without providing enough value.</p>\n<p>Open-backed shelving can sometimes separate functions while allowing more light through, but it should remain organized because visual clutter can make the entire room feel busier.</p>\n<h2>Use Glass and Transparent Elements Carefully</h2>\n<p>Interior glass doors or transparent decorative elements can help borrowed light move between spaces when they are already part of the home or when a renovation is appropriate. Any structural change should be planned correctly and comply with safety requirements.</p>\n<p>On a smaller scale, transparent tables or light furniture can reduce visual heaviness. They do not create more daylight, but they may help a compact room feel less crowded because fewer solid surfaces interrupt views.</p>\n<p>Always prioritize safety, especially with glass around children, pets, circulation routes, and doors.</p>\n<h2>Trim Outdoor Obstructions Responsibly</h2>\n<p>Vegetation outside a window can provide valuable shade, privacy, and cooling, but overgrown branches may reduce daylight more than intended. Where plants are on your property and pruning is appropriate, careful maintenance may restore a better balance.</p>\n<p>Do not remove healthy vegetation simply for more light without considering heat, privacy, wildlife, and seasonal benefits. Some trees provide useful summer shade and allow more light through in winter.</p>\n<p>For large trees or difficult pruning, use qualified professionals rather than attempting unsafe work.</p>\n<h2>Keep Window Glass and Frames Clean</h2>\n<p>Clean glass allows light to enter more effectively and makes the room feel better maintained. Establish a realistic schedule based on local dust, rain, pollution, and accessibility.</p>\n<p>Clean frames, tracks, and nearby surfaces as well. Dirt around the window can make the opening look darker even when the glass itself is clear.</p>\n<p>Use products suitable for the window material and follow manufacturer guidance, especially for coated or specialized glass.</p>\n<h2>Control Glare Instead of Blocking All Light</h2>\n<p>Strong daylight can create glare on television screens, computer monitors, or glossy worktops. The solution does not always need to be closing heavy curtains completely. Adjustable blinds, sheer curtains, screen placement, or a small furniture shift may solve the problem while preserving brightness.</p>\n<p>Observe when glare occurs. If it is limited to one or two hours, a flexible solution is more useful than permanently darkening the room all day.</p>\n<p>Task areas should be comfortable as well as bright. Natural light is valuable only when it supports how the room is actually used.</p>\n<h2>Use Interior Doors to Share Light</h2>\n<p>When privacy and acoustics allow, leaving interior doors open during the day can help brighter spaces share light with darker corridors or adjacent rooms. This is especially useful in homes where one side receives much more daylight than another.</p>\n<p>Do not block fire doors or ignore safety requirements. The idea applies only where ordinary interior doors can remain open safely and practically.</p>\n<p>Light sharing works best when connecting spaces use compatible colors and uncluttered sight lines.</p>\n<h2>Make Small Rooms Feel Brighter</h2>\n<p>In compact rooms, too many objects near the window can quickly reduce brightness. Keep tall storage away from the strongest light path when possible and use vertical storage on walls that do not interfere with the opening.</p>\n<p>Limit unnecessary dark, bulky pieces if the room already receives little daylight. This is not about removing personality but about balancing visual weight.</p>\n<p>Keeping some surfaces clear also allows light to reflect across the room rather than being absorbed by a dense collection of objects.</p>\n<h2>Plan Artificial Lighting Around Daylight</h2>\n<p>Even a well-lit home needs artificial lighting at night and on cloudy days. Use natural light to reduce unnecessary daytime use, but design artificial lighting so it supports the room when daylight fades.</p>\n<p>Task lights, floor lamps, and layered lighting can be more flexible than relying on one strong ceiling fixture for every activity.</p>\n<p>Switch lights off when daylight is sufficient, but do not compromise safety in stairs, work areas, or rooms where visibility is important.</p>\n<h2>Review the Room at Different Times</h2>\n<p>A room can change dramatically between morning and afternoon. Walk through the home at several times on a normal day and note where light enters, where shadows form, and when glare becomes uncomfortable.</p>\n<p>Take simple photographs from the same position at different times. Comparing them can reveal patterns that are easy to miss when relying on memory.</p>\n<p>Use those observations before buying curtains, moving large furniture, or changing paint colors. Evidence from your own room is more useful than a generic rule.</p>\n<h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>\n<h3>Do white walls always make a room brighter?</h3>\n<p>Light colors usually reflect more light, but the final effect depends on orientation, finishes, furniture, and the actual amount of daylight. Other pale colors can work very well too.</p>\n<h3>Should furniture stay away from every window?</h3>\n<p>Not necessarily. Low or appropriately scaled furniture can sit near windows without blocking much light. The important point is to preserve useful glass area and comfortable access.</p>\n<h3>Can mirrors increase natural light?</h3>\n<p>Mirrors can reflect existing light and views, making brightness feel more distributed. Placement matters because they can also reflect clutter or create glare.</p>\n<h3>What is the easiest first change?</h3>\n<p>Clear bulky objects away from the window, clean the glass, and adjust curtains or blinds so more of the opening is exposed during the day.</p>\n<h3>How can I improve light without losing privacy?</h3>\n<p>Consider adjustable blinds, sheer curtains, layered window treatments, or privacy solutions that allow daylight while limiting direct views.</p>\n<h2>Conclusion</h2>\n<p>Making better use of natural light is mainly about observing how daylight enters the home and removing unnecessary obstacles. Clean windows, appropriate curtains, well-positioned furniture, and carefully chosen surfaces can noticeably improve a room. The best solution respects privacy, glare, temperature, circulation, and the way each space is used. Small adjustments made from real observation often produce better results than major decorative changes.</p><h2>Create a Simple Daylight Checklist</h2><p>Once a month or when seasons change, check whether furniture has drifted in front of windows, curtains still open fully, plants have grown into the light path, and glass needs cleaning. Small changes accumulate slowly, so a quick review can restore brightness without spending money.</p><p>Also note which rooms still require artificial light during bright daytime hours. That observation can help you decide whether the issue is window access, layout, color, or simply the orientation of the home.</p><h2>Create a Simple Daylight Checklist</h2><p>Once a month or when seasons change, check whether furniture has drifted in front of windows, curtains still open fully, plants have grown into the light path, and glass needs cleaning. Small changes accumulate slowly, so a quick review can restore brightness without spending money.</p><p>Also note which rooms still require artificial light during bright daytime hours. That observation can help you decide whether the issue is window access, layout, color, or simply the orientation of the home.</p><h2>Create a Simple Daylight Checklist</h2><p>Once a month or when seasons change, check whether furniture has drifted in front of windows, curtains still open fully, plants have grown into the light path, and glass needs cleaning. Small changes accumulate slowly, so a quick review can restore brightness without spending money.</p><p>Also note which rooms still require artificial light during bright daytime hours. That observation can help you decide whether the issue is window access, layout, color, or simply the orientation of the home.</p>'}, {'filename': 'como-aprovechar-las-esquinas-de-la-casa.html', 'title': 'How to Make the Most of Corners at Home and Turn Them Into Useful Spaces', 'desc': 'Discover practical ideas for turning overlooked corners into useful spaces for reading, storage, work, plants, lighting, and decoration without creating clutter.', 'intro': 'Corners often become forgotten areas because they seem difficult to furnish. With accurate measurements and a clear purpose, they can become useful and attractive parts of the home.', 'category': 'HOME', 'body': "<h2>In This Article</h2>\n<p>Corners are often ignored because they seem awkward to furnish, but a well-measured corner can become storage, a compact workspace, a reading area, a plant location, or a simple decorative focal point. The key is deciding what the room genuinely needs before adding furniture.</p>\n<h2>Introduction</h2>\n<p>Corners are often ignored because they seem awkward to furnish, but a well-measured corner can become storage, a compact workspace, a reading area, a plant location, or a simple decorative focal point. The key is deciding what the room genuinely needs before adding furniture. The most effective improvements usually come from observing the room at different times of day, removing unnecessary obstacles, and making small changes that support daily use without creating new clutter.</p>\n<h2>Measure the Corner Before Deciding Its Function</h2>\n<p>An unused corner can look larger or smaller than it really is. Measure both walls, available depth, ceiling height, nearby doors, windows, outlets, radiators, and circulation space before choosing furniture.</p>\n<p>Do not decide that a corner needs storage simply because it is empty. First identify what the room is missing: seating, a work surface, books, lighting, display space, or perhaps nothing at all.</p>\n<p>A clear purpose prevents the corner from becoming a collection of random furniture that reduces movement.</p>\n<h2>Create a Small Reading Corner</h2>\n<p>A comfortable chair, suitable light, and a small surface for a book or drink may be enough to turn an overlooked corner into a useful reading area.</p>\n<p>Choose a chair that fits the available depth without projecting excessively into the room. If floor space is tight, a wall-mounted shelf can replace a side table.</p>\n<p>Use a floor lamp or wall light where electrical installation is safe and practical. The corner should feel inviting without creating cables across circulation routes.</p>\n<h2>Build a Compact Work Area</h2>\n<p>A corner can support a small desk when the room needs a dedicated place for occasional work or study. Measure legroom, chair movement, and screen distance rather than judging the desk only by its width.</p>\n<p>Keep the work area simple. A narrow desk, one shelf, and organized cable management may be more useful than a large workstation that overwhelms the room.</p>\n<p>If the area is used for long periods, prioritize ergonomics, suitable lighting, ventilation, and a chair that supports the task.</p>\n<h2>Use Corner Shelving Carefully</h2>\n<p>Corner shelves can add storage or display space without using much floor area, but they should not cover the walls from floor to ceiling automatically.</p>\n<p>Store frequently used objects at comfortable heights and keep heavier items on stable, appropriate supports. Wall-mounted shelving must be installed correctly for the wall construction and expected load.</p>\n<p>Leave some empty space between decorative objects so the corner remains visually calm.</p>\n<h2>Add a Plant When Conditions Are Suitable</h2>\n<p>A plant can soften an empty corner and add height without introducing heavy furniture. Choose the species according to the actual light, temperature, and humidity of the location.</p>\n<p>Dark corners may not provide enough light for many plants. Instead of repeatedly replacing struggling plants, choose a species suited to the conditions or use the corner for another purpose.</p>\n<p>Select a stable container and protect the floor from moisture.</p>\n<h2>Use a Corner for Closed Storage</h2>\n<p>In rooms where storage is genuinely needed, a narrow cabinet or corner unit can use vertical space efficiently. Measure door clearance and make sure the unit does not block nearby movement.</p>\n<p>Closed storage is useful for visually irregular items, but it should remain easy to access. A cabinet packed with forgotten objects does not improve organization.</p>\n<p>Secure tall furniture appropriately, especially in homes with children or in areas where tipping could be a risk.</p>\n<h2>Create a Decorative Focal Point</h2>\n<p>Not every corner needs a practical function. A piece of art, sculpture, floor lamp, or carefully chosen plant can provide visual balance without adding storage.</p>\n<p>Use one strong element rather than several unrelated small objects. This keeps the area intentional and reduces cleaning.</p>\n<p>Consider how the corner looks from the main entrance to the room and from nearby seating areas.</p>\n<h2>Use Mirrors With Purpose</h2>\n<p>A mirror can brighten a corner by reflecting existing light or an attractive view. Before mounting it, stand where it will hang and check what it will actually reflect.</p>\n<p>Avoid reflecting clutter, exposed cables, or a television unless that is intentional. The mirror should improve the visual experience rather than simply fill wall space.</p>\n<p>Use safe mounting methods suitable for the mirror's weight and wall type.</p>\n<h2>Create a Small Dining Nook</h2>\n<p>In some kitchens or open-plan rooms, a corner may support a compact breakfast area. A small table with a bench or two chairs can work if circulation remains comfortable.</p>\n<p>Measure chair pull-out space and nearby cabinet or appliance doors. A table that fits while empty may become impractical once people are seated.</p>\n<p>Choose proportions according to everyday household use rather than rare maximum capacity.</p>\n<h2>Turn a Corner Into an Entry Drop Zone</h2>\n<p>Near an entrance, a small corner can hold hooks, a slim shelf, or compact shoe storage. The goal is to give everyday items a defined place before they spread into the rest of the home.</p>\n<p>Keep the solution shallow so it does not narrow the entrance. Wall-mounted options can preserve floor space where appropriate.</p>\n<p>Limit the number of items stored there and review it regularly.</p>\n<h2>Use Corners in Bedrooms</h2>\n<p>A bedroom corner may support a reading chair, compact vanity, small desk, plant, or storage depending on the room's priorities.</p>\n<p>Preserve clear routes around the bed and wardrobes. Do not add furniture that makes drawers difficult to open or turns routine movement into an obstacle course.</p>\n<p>Choose one function and support it well instead of trying to combine several activities in a very small area.</p>\n<h2>Use Corners in Living Rooms</h2>\n<p>Living-room corners often work well for lighting, plants, books, or occasional seating. Consider what the room already has before adding another large piece.</p>\n<p>A floor lamp can fill vertical space without taking much floor area, while a narrow bookshelf can support storage if the room genuinely needs it.</p>\n<p>Keep the corner visually connected to the rest of the room through color, material, or style.</p>\n<h2>Use Kitchen Corners More Efficiently</h2>\n<p>Kitchen corners can be difficult because cabinet doors, worktops, and appliances compete for space. Prioritize accessibility over maximum storage capacity.</p>\n<p>Inside cabinets, rotating or pull-out organizers may help in some layouts, but they should fit the cabinet correctly and be durable enough for regular use.</p>\n<p>Keep frequently used cookware and food where it can be reached without emptying half the cabinet.</p>\n<h2>Avoid Overfilling the Corner</h2>\n<p>An empty corner is not automatically wasted space. In a room that already contains enough furniture, leaving a corner open can improve circulation and make the layout feel calmer.</p>\n<p>Do not force a tall shelf, plant, basket, lamp, and chair into the same small area. Choose the element that solves the most important need.</p>\n<p>Open space can be a deliberate design decision.</p>\n<h2>Use Vertical Height Thoughtfully</h2>\n<p>High corners may offer room for shelving, art, or tall plants, but height should support the composition rather than create visual heaviness.</p>\n<p>Keep frequently used items within safe reach. High shelves are better suited to lightweight or rarely used objects.</p>\n<p>Consider the ceiling, lighting, and any vents before adding tall furniture.</p>\n<h2>Plan Lighting for the Corner</h2>\n<p>A corner away from windows can feel gloomy even when the rest of the room is bright. A floor lamp, wall light, or nearby table lamp may make the area more usable.</p>\n<p>Choose lighting according to the activity. Reading needs different illumination from decorative ambience.</p>\n<p>Keep cables controlled and avoid creating trip hazards.</p>\n<h2>Review Corners as Needs Change</h2>\n<p>A corner that works as a home office today may become a reading area or storage zone later. Flexible furniture makes these changes easier.</p>\n<p>Periodically ask whether the corner still serves a real function. Removing an unnecessary piece can improve the room more than adding something new.</p>\n<p>Treat corners as adaptable parts of the home rather than permanent assignments.</p>\n<h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>\n<h3>What is the best use for an empty corner?</h3>\n<p>The best use depends on what the room lacks. Measure the area, identify a real need, and choose one clear function such as seating, storage, work, lighting, or decoration.</p>\n<h3>Are corner shelves always a good idea?</h3>\n<p>No. They are useful when storage or display is genuinely needed, but too many shelves can make a room feel crowded. Install them safely and keep the arrangement simple.</p>\n<h3>Can I create a home office in a corner?</h3>\n<p>Yes, if there is enough desk depth, chair movement, lighting, and ergonomic comfort. A very small corner may be better for occasional tasks than full-time work.</p>\n<h3>What if the corner is very dark?</h3>\n<p>Use appropriate artificial lighting or choose a function that does not depend on daylight. Do not place light-demanding plants there unless conditions are suitable.</p>\n<h3>Is it okay to leave a corner empty?</h3>\n<p>Absolutely. Open space supports circulation and visual calm. An unused corner is only a problem if the room genuinely needs a function there.</p>\n<h2>Conclusion</h2>\n<p>Using corners well begins with measurement and purpose. A corner can become a useful reading area, compact desk, storage zone, plant location, or decorative feature, but it does not need to contain something simply because it is empty. Preserve circulation, choose correctly scaled furniture, install wall-mounted pieces safely, and review the function as household needs change. A well-planned corner should improve the room rather than make it feel fuller.</p><h2>Test the Corner Before Buying Furniture</h2><p>Use cardboard, painter's tape, or an existing chair to mark the approximate footprint of a proposed piece. Leave it in place for a few days and notice whether people bump into it, doors still open comfortably, and the room remains easy to clean.</p><p>This temporary test is especially valuable in small homes because a few centimeters can change circulation significantly. Buying after testing reduces the risk of bringing home furniture that technically fits but does not function well.</p><h2>Test the Corner Before Buying Furniture</h2><p>Use cardboard, painter's tape, or an existing chair to mark the approximate footprint of a proposed piece. Leave it in place for a few days and notice whether people bump into it, doors still open comfortably, and the room remains easy to clean.</p><p>This temporary test is especially valuable in small homes because a few centimeters can change circulation significantly. Buying after testing reduces the risk of bringing home furniture that technically fits but does not function well.</p><h2>Test the Corner Before Buying Furniture</h2><p>Use cardboard, painter's tape, or an existing chair to mark the approximate footprint of a proposed piece. Leave it in place for a few days and notice whether people bump into it, doors still open comfortably, and the room remains easy to clean.</p><p>This temporary test is especially valuable in small homes because a few centimeters can change circulation significantly. Buying after testing reduces the risk of bringing home furniture that technically fits but does not function well.</p><h2>Test the Corner Before Buying Furniture</h2><p>Use cardboard, painter's tape, or an existing chair to mark the approximate footprint of a proposed piece. Leave it in place for a few days and notice whether people bump into it, doors still open comfortably, and the room remains easy to clean.</p><p>This temporary test is especially valuable in small homes because a few centimeters can change circulation significantly. Buying after testing reduces the risk of bringing home furniture that technically fits but does not function well.</p><h2>Test the Corner Before Buying Furniture</h2><p>Use cardboard, painter's tape, or an existing chair to mark the approximate footprint of a proposed piece. Leave it in place for a few days and notice whether people bump into it, doors still open comfortably, and the room remains easy to clean.</p><p>This temporary test is especially valuable in small homes because a few centimeters can change circulation significantly. Buying after testing reduces the risk of bringing home furniture that technically fits but does not function well.</p><h2>Test the Corner Before Buying Furniture</h2><p>Use cardboard, painter's tape, or an existing chair to mark the approximate footprint of a proposed piece. Leave it in place for a few days and notice whether people bump into it, doors still open comfortably, and the room remains easy to clean.</p><p>This temporary test is especially valuable in small homes because a few centimeters can change circulation significantly. Buying after testing reduces the risk of bringing home furniture that technically fits but does not function well.</p>"}]

def wc(html):
    return len(re.findall(r"\b[\w'-]+\b", re.sub(r"<[^>]+>", " ", html)))

print("=" * 68)
print("BLOCK 03 - ARTICLES 07 AND 08")
print("=" * 68)

for d in DATA:
    words = wc(d["body"])
    if not 2000 <= words <= 3000:
        print(f'ERROR - {d["filename"]}: {words} words')
        raise SystemExit(1)

    path = ART / d["filename"]
    if not path.exists():
        print("ERROR - file not found:", d["filename"])
        raise SystemExit(1)

for d in DATA:
    path = ART / d["filename"]
    text = path.read_text(encoding="utf-8", errors="replace")

    backup = BACK / d["filename"]
    if not backup.exists():
        shutil.copy2(path, backup)

    text = re.sub(r'<html\s+lang=["\']es["\']', '<html lang="en"', text, flags=re.I)

    text = re.sub(
        r'(<h1[^>]*>).*?(</h1>)',
        lambda m: m.group(1) + d["title"] + m.group(2),
        text, count=1, flags=re.I | re.S
    )

    text = re.sub(
        r'(<title[^>]*>).*?(</title>)',
        lambda m: m.group(1) + d["title"] + " | Home & Garden" + m.group(2),
        text, count=1, flags=re.I | re.S
    )

    text = re.sub(
        r'(<meta[^>]+name=["\']description["\'][^>]+content=["\'])[^"\']*(["\'])',
        lambda m: m.group(1) + d["desc"] + m.group(2),
        text, count=1, flags=re.I | re.S
    )

    text = re.sub(
        r'(<p[^>]+class=["\'][^"\']*article-intro[^"\']*["\'][^>]*>).*?(</p>)',
        lambda m: m.group(1) + d["intro"] + m.group(2),
        text, count=1, flags=re.I | re.S
    )

    text = re.sub(
        r'(<span[^>]+class=["\'][^"\']*article-category[^"\']*["\'][^>]*>).*?(</span>)',
        lambda m: m.group(1) + d["category"] + m.group(2),
        text, count=1, flags=re.I | re.S
    )

    pattern = r'(<article\b[^>]*class=["\'][^"\']*article-content-full[^"\']*["\'][^>]*>).*?(</article>)'
    text, n = re.subn(
        pattern,
        lambda m: m.group(1) + "\n" + d["body"] + "\n" + m.group(2),
        text,
        count=1,
        flags=re.I | re.S
    )

    if n != 1:
        print("ERROR - article-content-full not found:", d["filename"])
        raise SystemExit(1)

    common = {
        "Casa & Jardín": "Home & Garden",
        "Publicado:": "Published:",
        "Actualizado:": "Updated:",
        "Inicio": "Home",
        "Categorías": "Categories",
        "Sobre nosotros": "About Us",
        "Contacto": "Contact",
        "Compartir": "Share",
        "Artículos recientes": "Recent Articles",
        "También te puede interesar": "You May Also Like",
        "HOGAR": "HOME",
        "Equipo Editorial de Casa & Jardín": "Home & Garden Editorial Team",
        "Ideas y consejos sobre hogar, decoración, organización y jardinería.": "Ideas and practical advice about the home, decor, organization, and gardening."
    }

    for old, new in common.items():
        text = text.replace(old, new)

    path.write_text(text, encoding="utf-8")
    print(f'OK - {d["filename"]} -> {wc(d["body"])} words')

